%% Figure 2: Application Request and Response Lifecycle
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    actor User as End User (Browser)
    participant IGW as Internet Gateway
    participant SG as EC2 Security Group (Port 80)
    participant Host as EC2 Host (Port 80)
    participant Docker as Docker Container (Port 5000)
    participant Gunicorn as Gunicorn WSGI Server
    participant Flask as Flask Application (run:app)
    participant RDS as Amazon RDS MySQL (Port 3306)

    User->>IGW: HTTP GET /dashboard (TCP 80)
    IGW->>SG: Inbound Packet Inspection
    SG->>Host: Allow Inbound Port 80 Traffic
    Host->>Docker: Port Forward 0.0.0.0:80 -> 5000/tcp
    Docker->>Gunicorn: Forward to WSGI Worker Process
    Gunicorn->>Flask: WSGI Environment & Request Dispatch

    Note over Flask: Session Validation & Route Handling (/dashboard)
    Flask->>RDS: SQL Query: SELECT transactions, budgets FROM user_id
    Note over RDS: Execute query inside Private Subnet (Port 3306)
    RDS-->>Flask: Return Financial Query Result Set

    Note over Flask: Compute Income ($1,200), Expenses ($205), Net ($995)<br/>Render HTML via Jinja2 Template
    Flask-->>Gunicorn: HTTP 200 OK + Rendered HTML Payload
    Gunicorn-->>Docker: Output Stream
    Docker-->>Host: Bridged Response
    Host-->>IGW: Outbound Response Packet
    IGW-->>User: Render Dashboard in Browser Window
